eagues you know I'm talking you know  probably in their sixties kind of at the  towards the ends of their career I was  never treated badly by them either and  so you know there's so many factors I'm  just I'm not entirely sure which one it  was sure no that makes sense in terms of  the work that you did at the FBI number  one where did where did you specialize  there and how did it feel to be doing  that versus what you had been working on  in counterterrorism at the CIA so yeah I  mean it was initially surprising to me  that they placed me in Chinese  counterintelligence I had assumed that  I'd be working counterterrorism simply  because that's what I had done  for the past five years or so of the  agency but I didn't approach it with a  negative attitude I'm kind of the person  that's like well let's just go find out  more about this and you know it I was  looking for it was surprised but still  looking forward to it so I was placed it  in the Chinese counterintelligence  school and it turns out that they had  put me there not because of my expertise  but because of my security clearance  that I had at the CIA when you think  back to the work you did at both  agencies do you have a strong intuitive  sense that what you did at one versus  the other was of more sort of direct  value or more rewarding in some sense  that's a really good question and I'm  not sure that I can give you a fair  answer because at the FBI my impact or  my thought of any impact that I had is  severely clouded by my treatment right  as a female so I don't know that that's  fair in terms of like face to face  impact yeah the FBI definitely had more  because you're dealing so much with with  victims and families and those kinds of  things at the CIA I felt like I had a  tremendous impact but it's much more  nebulous and more more broad when um  when we hear about the sometimes lack of  playing nice as it's called between some  intelligence agencies and obviously with  the current president there's been a lot  of conflict also between the agencies  and the White House as well do you have  any kind of particular insight from the  time you spent at both agencies and  relating to the people in the White  House about what is going on if there  are particular dynamics that really vary  from administration to administration or  if there's something about the structure  of these agencies that leads to this  type of conflict so I think it's like a  two two pronged answer I think the first  one is I worked there under Bush and I  worked there under Clinton so obviously  two ideologically different presidents  both of them kept Tennant as the head of  the CIA and I would say both of them you  know regardless of what you think  politically I'm just reporting how I  felt  treated intelligence in a except for  later on in a rather  a political manner you know the  Intelligence Agency was not there just  to serve them or you know dig up dirt  both of them read the pub the  presidential Daily Brief both of them  would have questions about the  presidential Daily Brief so I  appreciated that um in working there my  colleagues were very apolitical I mean  we just didn't I think now unfortunately  what we're seeing is that it's the  intelligence community's being used as  as protective arm for the president and  I think that is a huge problem  regardless of where you stand  politically I wish people could sort of  get politics out of their mind and just  kind of look back and see why this is  negative I think from why these two  agencies dislike each other so much has  to do with their missions  the CIA is an intelligence gathering  organization that's all they do that is  their mission the FBI is a  law-enforcement organization and I think  the CIA because they're dealing with  human assets that's a different security  clearance and the FBI gets their  feathers ruffled when the CIA says oh I  can't tell you that and so I think  that's where you it's this clash of ego  that sort of happens and that's a huge  problem I think we need to sort of step  back from ourselves and our egos and  realize that this is sometimes for the  greater good of national security  last thing I wanted to ask you about now  that you are out of that world are you  are there things that still affect you  from it in terms of whether you think  about your personal security on a day to  day basis or in any other way  so I'm there's been a kind of three  different things the first one was I had  to un testify in front of the Inspector  General about torture and things like  that when I was already out of the  agency so you know clearly there was an  issue yes uh the second one was I  believe it was a data breach or data  hack in the Office of Personnel  Management and they had all of our  information um people who had worked  there and then the third is since the  release of my book there's been a lot of  people that really don't like that I  worked for the CIA  um and that I threatened me  um yelled at me screamed at me called me  a murderer until now you know our house  is under close watch from our local  police department so that's been an  issue when you say people have yelled at  you people have found you in the real  world and yelled at you yes so you know  I'd have a book signing events people  will yell at me there or how people  follow me to private events held at me  there it's quite interesting and when  you say that there they're mad you work  that the CIA like what does that even  mean they they're mad well and I and I'm  not I don't mean to be political I'm at  I'm actually an independent politically  but um what had happened was is  unfortunately my books release came out  at the same time as there was a hashtag  trending abolish the CIA and it was  because of some things I think from the  Bernie Sanders time that he had served  and people were showing up to chant that  and call me a murderer for have working  there for housing oh yeah Wow it's an  incredible story we've been speaking  with Tracy Walder her book is the  unexpected spy which we are linking to  Tracy I can't tell you how much I  appreciate your time and you sharing  your story with us today thank you so  much for having me I really enjoyed this  very much
 

